---
const tradeoffs = [
  {
    option: "A website builder (Squarespace, Wix)",
    gives: "He could edit it himself.",
    costs: "It would make him look like every other contractor, and I couldn't tune it to the premium feel his work deserved.",
  },
  {
    option: "A custom site from a freelancer",
    gives: "It would look bespoke.",
    costs: "He'd be calling that freelancer, and paying, every time he wanted to post a new job.",
  },
];
---

<!-- Why I Built Instead of Bought -->
<section class="mb-24">
  <div class="mb-8">
    <span
      class="text-xs font-semibold text-amber-700 tracking-widest uppercase"
    >
      03 — Why I Built Instead of Bought
    </span>
    <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-2">
      The off-the-shelf options each made a trade I wasn't willing to make.
    </h2>
  </div>

  <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-5 mb-8">
    {
      tradeoffs.map((t) => (
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-gray-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
          <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-semibold text-lg mb-3">{t.option}</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 mb-2">
            <span class="font-semibold text-violet-700">Gives him: </span>
            {t.gives}
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">
            <span class="font-semibold text-amber-700">Costs him: </span>
            {t.costs}
          </p>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <div class="space-y-5 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed text-lg max-w-3xl">
    <p>
      Editable but generic, or custom but dependent. I wanted both: the polish
      of a bespoke build and the autonomy of a tool he could run himself.
    </p>
    <p>
      And the purpose-built tools do exist. I looked at them. CompanyCam will
      even host a project gallery on your own domain, filterable by type of
      work. Houzz Pro builds contractor sites outright. I built anyway, because
      for a brand selling five-figure work a templated gallery undercuts the
      pitch, and I wanted to own the things that actually mattered here: the
      design, the performance, and an upload flow shaped around one specific
      person instead of a generic contractor. Buying would have been faster.
      Building let me control every decision that follows.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
